WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.559
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: The report

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:31.119
covers a lot of areas; I am just going to
pull out a handful that I think are worth

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:42.340
noting. Starting with urgent care, four-hour
wait target: we remained at just over 90%

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:48.290
achieving that target, which has been pretty
much consistent for the last four&nbsp;months.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:58.431
We are still missing the 95% target. Admissions
have been up 3.5% in the last month, and up

00:00:58.431 --> 00:01:06.820
around 4% over the past year; so whilst performance
is good by international standards, it is

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.540
still below what we are expecting and what
we require.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.200
To that end, we are now rolling out the Urgent
and Emergency Care Plan that I think we have

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:24.810
discussed before, to put us in a good place
for winter, which has some key elements to

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:31.510
it. Partly, it is around extending integrated
urgent care, so it covers ambulance services,

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:37.880
111, primary care and out-of-hours services,
to enable patients to access the right services

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:44.979
at the right time. Part of it is about streaming
patients when they come into the A&amp;E department;

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.950
still two thirds of patients who come into
an A&amp;E department have travelled under their

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:54.530
own steam, they have not been sent by a GP
or brought by an ambulance, and a significant

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:01.970
proportion of those people do not need the
care that A&amp;E can provide, so we really need

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:07.780
to make sure that we are diverting patients
and streaming them to the right services.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.150
Then we need to improve the smoothness of
flow through hospitals, so there is a focus

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:21.750
on the management of patients through: neither
discharging patients too early, nor keeping

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:27.420
patients in hospital longer than they need
to be, and delayed transfers of care remains

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:37.340
a significant problem. In July, over 6,000
patients were delayed transferred; the highest

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:45.010
since records began – we started counting
in this way in 2010 – and 25% higher than

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.750
it was this time last year. To put that in
context, that is effectively two hospitals’

00:02:49.750 --> 00:03:00.099
worth of additional delayed transfers that
the NHS is looking after. So a lot of work

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:06.410
going on in urgent care; a lot of elements
to the solution, and I think commissioners

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.220
and providers are working together to address
those.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:19.510
In elective care, referral-to-treat standard:
we remained at just over 91%; over the last

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:28.110
five months, that has been pretty consistent.
Just a little below the 92% standards. Patients

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:38.189
completing treatment was 5% up in July, and
is 4% up compared to the previous 12 months.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:46.860
The NHS is doing more work than ever, but
hospitals are doing less inpatient activity

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:52.360
than they have been contracted to, than we
are paying them to do. So I am very hopeful

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.890
that, with the junior doctors’ dispute coming
to an end, we will now see doctors ramping

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:07.299
up their activity, driving down waiting times
and seeing the improvement in standards that

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:09.510
we are looking for.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:19.070
In cancer we hit the waiting times measures,
except for the 62-day standard; we were at

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:25.340
82‑and-a-bit percent against an 85% target.
That reflects a shortage of diagnostic capacity

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:28.389
that we are in the process of addressing.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:36.939
I would like to touch on a couple of the less
focused areas, which I think we should consider

00:04:36.939 --> 00:04:45.289
to be just as important. We continued to hit
the standard for the speed of diagnosis of

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:49.550
patients with dementia, which is absolutely
vitally important for those patients, to be

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:54.839
getting them into the right care and the right
support that they need, and so the NHS is

00:04:54.839 --> 00:05:00.330
doing a good job there. The total number of
patients with learning disabilities who are

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:06.020
in hospital and who could be being managed
in more appropriate settings and getting back

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:11.699
freedom into their lives has continued to
fall, and that is a focus across the NHS at

00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:16.389
the moment, to make sure that that group of
patients gets the care that they need.

00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:20.939
So work going on across the board, but we
have some real work to do, particularly on

00:05:20.939 --> 00:05:27.379
the urgent care standards and elective access,
to get ourselves up to where we need to be.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:46.200
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: I was not going to talk to
the main report; it is a normal update on

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:54.249
our corporate programmes and our corporate
risks. However, I just want to flag one risk

00:05:54.249 --> 00:06:02.319
that we are reporting this time, and a performance
issue which relates to primary care services.

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:10.130
As you know, we contracted with Capita to
run those support services in September last

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:20.679
year. After the first few months, where we
had good service, Capita started to hit problems

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:29.919
with the way that they delivered those services,
and that has escalated over the summer, so

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:40.280
we do have some unacceptable levels of performance
in primary care support. We and Capita both

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:47.849
think this is unacceptable, and are committed
to recovering those services, and are working

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:52.719
to do that, and we are working very closely
with them to ensure that they have good recovery

00:06:52.719 --> 00:07:01.899
plans and do start the hit the targets and
service expectations that we set for them.

00:07:01.899 --> 00:07:10.179
I just want to recognise that obviously this
has impacts for users, which include, of course,

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:17.319
primary care contractors, GPs, ophthalmic
and dental practitioners, and recognise that

00:07:17.319 --> 00:07:24.479
that is difficult for all those users, and
indeed creating some risks for patients as

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:33.059
well. We are doing everything we can to make
sure that we escalate and address the risks,

00:07:33.059 --> 00:07:39.869
particularly for patients, and we will be
communicating with practitioners how we are

00:07:39.869 --> 00:07:45.669
planning on, with Capita, trying to improve
services as quickly as possible.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:53.459
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen. As you
know, the Board takes this extremely gravely:

00:07:53.459 --> 00:07:58.589
when we say unacceptable, we mean unacceptable.
I think we need to pay tribute to you and

00:07:58.589 --> 00:08:03.699
the team for the huge amount of work and effort
that has gone in to trying to ensure that

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:10.869
people are not harmed by this at all, and
to assure you that we, the Board will be tracking

00:08:10.869 --> 00:08:16.080
this as closely as possible over the weeks.
We need recovery from this as quickly as we

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:26.549
possibly can achieve it.
We note the report.

